Running MIMEDefang as non-root
------------------------------

It is required to run MIMEDefang and the multiplexor as non-root.  You
should create a dedicated user for MIMEDefang.  In the examples, I'll
call this user "defang".

To run as defang:

1) Supply the "-U defang" option to mimedefang.

2) Supply the "-U defang" option to mimedefang-multiplexor.

3) Make the spool and quarantine directories owned by defang, with mode 700.

4) If you are using statistics logging in the default /var/log/mimedefang
directory, make that directory owned by defang with mode 755 or 700, depending
on your tastes.

5) Create the socket(s) in the spool directory.  As of MIMEDefang 2.13,
this is the default.  You may have to edit your sendmail.cf or sendmail.mc
file to use /var/spool/MIMEDefang instead of /var/run as the directory
containing the milter socket.

6) Do NOT put the pid files in the spool directory.  The daemons create
them as root, before dropping privileges, and init scripts trust their
contents ("kill `cat /var/run/mimedefang.pid`"); anybody who can write to
the directory holding a pid file can replace it.  Use a directory only
root can write to, such as /var/run, and use -o to put the lock files,
which are opened after privileges are dropped, in the spool directory:

  mimedefang-multiplexor -U defang -p /var/run/mimedefang-multiplexor.pid \
      -o /var/spool/MIMEDefang/mimedefang-multiplexor.lock ...
  mimedefang -U defang -P /var/run/mimedefang.pid \
      -o /var/spool/MIMEDefang/mimedefang.lock ...

Without -o the lock file defaults to <pidfile>.lock, which defang cannot
create in /var/run.  The sample init scripts already do this; the systemd
units do not use pid files.  The daemons refuse a pid file that is a
symbolic link, a hard link, not a regular file, or owned by another user.
